<!--
  Captured responses from the BNP and PORBASE URN resolvers.

  WHY THESE ARE COMMITTED: the app can never fetch them. Both hosts send no
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin header, so a browser cannot read these responses
  at all -- they were captured server-side, where CORS does not apply. Until
  that changes, these two documents are the only real examples of the format
  this project has, and js/network.js's parseUrnXml is written against them.
  If BNP changes its output or the service goes down, this file is the record.

  Captured 2026-08-31. Both are the same book, so the two catalogues can be
  compared directly:

      https://urn.bnportugal.gov.pt/isbn/mods/xml?id=978-972-41-2174-1
      https://urn.porbase.org/isbn/mods/xml?id=978-972-41-2174-1

  Request shape is /{scheme}/{schema}/{serialization}?id={value}. Dropping the
  schema/serialization segments -- as the app's original URLs did -- yields
  <error>Pedido invalido - nao contem forma</error>, not a record. A valid
  request with no match yields <error>Registo inexistente</error>. Both arrive
  with HTTP 200, so status codes cannot be used to tell success from failure.

  THREE THINGS TO NOTICE, all of which parseUrnXml depends on:

  1. There are no flat <author> or <date> elements. MODS nests everything:
     name > namePart, originInfo > dateIssued|dateOther. The parser that
     preceded this file guessed at a flat schema and would have returned an
     edition with no author and no year on every single lookup.

  2. <identifier type="stock">PT - 272507/08</identifier> IS the Deposito
     Legal -- confirmed against our own build, where BNP record 1731654
     carries deposito_legal = 272507/08. The shelf mark (cota) is separately
     in location > physicalLocation. It is deliberately NOT mapped by
     parseUrnXml: DL drives the dex ordinal, and BNP has not formally
     documented what "stock" means.

  3. PORBASE leaks UNIMARC non-filing-character markers into the title as
     "?A ?nuvem cor-de-rosa" (the markers wrap the article "A " so it sorts
     under "nuvem"), where BNP returns "A nuvem cor-de-rosa". Any parser must
     strip the paired markers without eating a genuine question mark.

  Note also <identifier type="uri">, which carries the persistent
  id.bnportugal.gov.pt URI -- the 100%-filled "Persistent URL" column that
  scripts/build_index.py currently does not store.

  See docs/bnp-findings.md (addendum) for the full write-up.
-->

